PLACES=[
N('fort-saybrook-roundhouse-old-saybrook','Fort Saybrook Monument Park and railroad roundhouse','Old Saybrook','ctsw','fort','traces',41.28468,-72.35034,'site','public','walk','forts,stations','1636–1922',
  'Lion Gardiner arrived in March 1636 to build a palisade fort and a windmill here at the river mouth for the Warwick patentees; the HMdb marker calls it the first military fortification in southern New England. Pequot warriors harassed it until the colonies declared war in 1637. The fort was used on and off for about 150 years, sold in 1808 and manned again as Fort Fenwick in the War of 1812. In 1870–71 the Connecticut Valley Railroad levelled what was left of the fort and built a roundhouse and turntable on it. The sources disagree on when the rail facilities ended: the marker says the railroad was abandoned in 1915 and the roundhouse pulled down, Wikipedia says the Saybrook Point extension stopped running in 1922.',
  'Nothing of the 1636 fort stands. What you can see are the excavated foundations of the six-bay quarter-round roundhouse (brick and stone) and the concrete base of the turntable, dug out in 1981–82 and left exposed, with interpretive signs and storyboards on the colony\'s history around the 17-acre park, most of it tidal marsh.',
  'The first English fort in Connecticut, buried under a railroad roundhouse.',
  'A public park on Saybrook Point maintained by the Fort Saybrook Monument Park Association (HMdb marker); the federal land map shows it as Fort Saybrook Park. DEEP\'s coastal access guide for Saybrook Point (2 Bridge Street) says parking is in the adjacent Fort Saybrook lot, free. Hours not checked.',
  [W('Connecticut Valley Railroad Roundhouse and Turntable Site'),['HMdb: Welcome to Fort Saybrook','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=182308'],['CT DEEP coastal access guide: Saybrook Point','https://portal.ct.gov/-/media/deep/gis/coastalaccessmaps/coastalaccesssite110.pdf'],['CTvisit: Fort Saybrook Monument Park','https://ctvisit.com/listings/fort-saybrook-monument-park']],
  conf='mixed',th=[41.28468,-72.35034],thn='Fort Saybrook Monument Park lot',brick='some'),
N('lynde-point-light-old-saybrook','Lynde Point Light','Old Saybrook','ctsw','light','standing',41.27161,-72.34339,'exact','view','drive','','1838–',
  'A 35-foot wooden tower was built on William Lynde\'s land at the river mouth in 1803. It was too short to be seen well, and in 1838 the present octagonal brownstone tower was built; it was lit in 1839. It burned whale oil until 1879, was electrified in 1955 and automated by the Coast Guard in 1978. The 1858 Gothic Revival keeper\'s house was torn down in 1966 for a duplex.',
  'The tapering brownstone tower, which the Register listing singles out for its stonework, with the 1966 keeper\'s duplex beside it. Saybrook Breakwater Light, a "sparkplug" light, stands on the jetty offshore and can be seen with it. The tower is not open.',
  '',
  'Not open to the public: New England Lighthouse Treasures calls it a private residence whose grounds are closed, reached by a private road through the Fenwick golf course, and suggests asking permission before stopping for photographs. A distant view is had from the shore and from boats on the river. Who owns it now was not checked.',
  [W('Lynde Point Light'),W('Saybrook Breakwater Light'),['New England Lighthouse Treasures: Lynde Point Light','https://nelights.com/exploring/Connecticut/lynde_point_light.html']],
  brick='no'),
N('shore-line-power-house-old-saybrook','Shore Line Electric Railway Power House','Old Saybrook','ctsw','rail','standing',41.31349,-72.36116,'site','view','drive','','1910–',
  'A coal-fired power station built in 1908–1910 for the Shore Line Electric Railway, an interurban trolley between New Haven and Old Saybrook that ran, on and off, from 1910 to 1929. It also fed the New London and East Lyme line and worked the railroad bridge over the river. It was sold in 1938 and became a yacht yard, then a boat repair shop.',
  'A two-storey, five-bay reinforced-concrete building with Classical and Arts and Crafts touches, big multi-light steel windows and a smokestack rising from the flat roof, facing the river on Clark Street. The Register form (2019) says it keeps its open interior volumes; the interior was not checked and is not open.',
  'The last big piece of a Connecticut trolley line that failed in under twenty years.',
  'On a private marina lot; the rest of the lot holds the marina office, boat racks and a repair shop (Register form). See it from the street only. Current use not checked.',
  [NRHP('100004086','Shore Line Electric Railway Power House'),W('Shore Line Electric Railway'),['Old Saybrook Historical Society: the Saybrook trolley (2022)','https://saybrookhistory.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/5-Saybrook-Trolley-Apr-22-1.pdf']],
  brick='no'),
N('connecticut-river-museum-essex','Connecticut River Museum (1878 steamboat warehouse)','Essex','ctsw','water','standing',41.3518,-72.3851,'exact','public','drive','','1878–',
  'A steamboat warehouse built in 1878 at Steamboat Dock, which Wikipedia calls the only one of its kind left on the river. It has been the Connecticut River Museum since 1975.',
  'The three-storey warehouse on the dock, with a permanent shipbuilding exhibit on the second floor and a full-size replica of the Turtle, the submarine built in Essex in 1776. Original fabric inside the warehouse was not checked.',
  '',
  'A private museum at 67 Main Street. CTvisit lists it open Tuesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. with paid admission (adults $12 when checked); the museum\'s own visit page answered 404, so hours were not checked with the museum. Parking at the foot of Main Street.',
  [W('Connecticut River Museum'),['CTvisit: Connecticut River Museum','https://ctvisit.com/listings/connecticut-river-museum'],['Connecticut River Museum','https://ctrivermuseum.org/']],
  th=[41.35193,-72.38516],thn='Lot at the foot of Main Street, Essex',brick='?'),
N('hills-academy-essex','Hill\'s Academy','Essex','ctsw','civic','standing',41.35389,-72.39278,'exact','public','drive','','1832–',
  'A Greek Revival school building of 1832 that served as an academy until the town took it over in 1902 and as a school until 1910. The Improved Order of Red Men used it as a lodge from 1915 to 1954, after which the Essex Historical Society was set up to take it on.',
  'A two-storey brick building with white-painted brick pilasters, a full pediment and a squat wooden belfry, looking down on Main Street from Prospect Street beside the Congregational and Baptist churches. Inside, narrow stairs wind up from the vestibule (Wikipedia). It is the historical society\'s home.',
  '',
  'Essex Historical Society, 22 Prospect Street. Its page gives drop-in hours on Tuesdays and Thursdays, 9 a.m. to noon, and by appointment; parking behind the building.',
  [W('Hill\'s Academy'),['Essex Historical Society: contact and hours','https://www.essexhistory.org/contact-us']],
  brick='?'),
N('ivoryton-playhouse-essex','Ivoryton Playhouse (Comstock-Cheney Hall)','Essex','ctsw','civic','standing',41.34861,-72.44222,'exact','view','drive','','1911–',
  'Built in 1910–11 as a recreation hall for the workers of Comstock, Cheney and Company, which made piano keys and actions from ivory and made Ivoryton a company town. It showed silent films and held dances and town meetings; from 1930 Milton Stiefel ran a summer stock company there, and it is held to be the oldest self-supporting summer theatre in the country. Katharine Hepburn played there early on.',
  'The Classical Revival hall in the middle of the Ivoryton Historic District, a preserved nineteenth-century company town. The interior was not checked; it is seen by buying a ticket to a show.',
  'The ivory company\'s dance hall that became America\'s first self-supporting summer theatre.',
  'A working theatre; the building is open for performances. Season and times not checked. Look at the outside from Main Street, Ivoryton.',
  [W('Comstock-Cheney Hall'),W('Ivoryton, Connecticut')],
  brick='?'),
N('old-town-hall-chester','Old Town Hall, Chester','Chester','ctsw','civic','standing',41.40694,-72.4525,'exact','view','drive','','1793–',
  'Built in 1793 as the meeting house of the local Congregational parish, then still part of Saybrook. The church moved out in 1845, and the town bought the building in 1847 for $300 for town meetings. In 1876 it was remodelled as a theatre; town meetings were held there until 1960.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey timber-frame building on the north end of Chester Green, clapboarded, with a steep roof carried on heavy shaped timber trusses and Federal entrances (no longer used) in both gable ends; single-storey wings hold the vestibule and the stage. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'On Chester Green between Liberty Street and Goose Hill Road. Who uses it now and whether it opens was not checked; look from the green.',
  [W('Old Town Hall (Chester, Connecticut)')],
  brick='no'),
N('gillette-castle-east-haddam','Gillette Castle','East Haddam','ctsw','landmark','standing',41.42222,-72.42861,'site','public','walk','','1914–',
  'The actor William Gillette, famous for playing Sherlock Holmes on stage, built this stone house, which he called Seventh Sister, on the southernmost of the Seven Sisters hills above the river between 1914 and 1919, with an addition in 1924. He lived here until he died in 1937. The state bought it in 1943 for $29,000 and made it a state park; it reopened in 2002 after a four-year restoration.',
  'The 14,000-square-foot stone castle 200 feet above the river. The rooms are seen on a self-guided tour of about an hour; what original fittings survive inside was not checked. The grounds have trails along the bed of Gillette\'s miniature railroad. The house itself stands in Lyme; the park address is East Haddam.',
  '',
  'Gillette Castle State Park, 67 River Road, East Haddam (CT DEEP). Grounds open daily 8 a.m. to sunset; parking and grounds free (Friends of the park). The castle interior opens only for scheduled tours: daily from Memorial Day weekend to Labor Day, weekends and holidays to Columbus Day, and Friday to Sunday from Thanksgiving to Christmas for the holiday program; the Friends give castle admission as $6 for those 13 and over, booked through Reserve America.',
  [W('Gillette Castle State Park'),GCSP,GCF],
  th=[41.42338,-72.42726],thn='Park lot off Geer Hill Road',brick='no'),
N('goodspeed-opera-house-east-haddam','Goodspeed Opera House','East Haddam','ctsw','civic','standing',41.45176,-72.46252,'exact','public','drive','','1876–',
  'Built by the merchant and banker William Henry Goodspeed in 1876–77, with a general store, meat market and post office on the ground floor and a theatre above; the first play opened in October 1877. After Goodspeed died in 1882 it declined, serving as a militia base in the First World War, a store and a state highway department depot. It was later restored; Goodspeed Musicals, where Annie and Man of La Mancha began, was founded in 1963 and plays there.',
  'The tall Victorian theatre on the riverbank beside the East Haddam bridge, one of the best-known views on the river. Inside, the staircase and theatre are seen on a free hour-long tour (backstage and dressing rooms) or by going to a show; original fabric not checked in detail.',
  '',
  'Goodspeed Musicals, East Haddam. Free tours on certain Saturdays at 10 a.m. (2026 dates on its tours page; RSVP asked). Otherwise seen from outside or at a performance.',
  [W('Goodspeed Musicals'),['Goodspeed Musicals: tours','https://www.goodspeed.org/visit/goodspeed-tours']],
  brick='?'),
N('chapman-falls-devils-hopyard','Chapman Falls and Beebe\'s mill site, Devil\'s Hopyard','East Haddam','ctsw','industry','traces',41.48371,-72.3418,'site','public','walk','','1775–1890s',
  'Chapman Falls drops more than sixty feet over steps of schist on the Eightmile River. The falls powered Beebe\'s Mills, named for their first owner, until the mid-1890s; Wikipedia says the Sons of Liberty attacked a loyalist-owned mill here in 1775, and a piece of a broken millstone found at the foot of the falls in 2002 is the subject of a park sign. The state took the land as a park in 1919.',
  'The falls and their potholes, the sign about the millstone, and Bridge No. 1603 on the main park road just north: a fieldstone arch of rounded cobbles finished in 1937 by Depression relief labour, one of three park bridges on the Register. What survives of the mill buildings was not found in any source.',
  'A loyalist\'s mill wrecked by the Sons of Liberty, found again as a broken millstone.',
  'Devil\'s Hopyard State Park, 366 Hopyard Road, East Haddam (CT DEEP): open daily 8 a.m. to sunset. Park in the lot on Hopyard Road by the falls.',
  [W('Devil\'s Hopyard State Park'),['CT DEEP: Devil\'s Hopyard State Park','https://portal.ct.gov/deep/state-parks/parks/devils-hopyard-state-park'],NRHP('93000641','Bridge No. 1603')],
  th=[41.48251,-72.34209],thn='Park lot on Hopyard Road near the falls',brick='no'),
N('amasa-day-house-moodus','Amasa Day House','East Haddam','ctsw','landmark','standing',41.50194,-72.4525,'exact','public','drive','','1816–',
  'A Federal house built in 1816 for the farmer Colonel Julius Chapman at the head of the Moodus green; Amasa Day bought it after Chapman died. Connecticut Landmarks owns it and uses it to show how the industrial revolution changed family life in a mill village.',
  'A clapboarded house with a gabled portico and half-round windows in its pedimented ends. Inside are Day family furnishings and the original nineteenth-century stencilling on the floors and stairs, painted to look like carpet (Connecticut Landmarks).',
  '',
  'Connecticut Landmarks, 33 Plains Road, Moodus. A "study house" open for tours by appointment, requested by email through its page; admission charged.',
  [W('Amasa Day House'),['Connecticut Landmarks: Amasa Day House','https://ctlandmarks.org/properties/amasa-day-house/']],
  brick='no'),
N('comstocks-covered-bridge-east-hampton','Comstock\'s Covered Bridge','East Hampton','ctsw','landmark','standing',41.55328,-72.44844,'exact','public','walk','','1873–',
  'A covered Howe-truss bridge over the Salmon River between East Hampton and Colchester, one of three historic covered bridges left in Connecticut. The sources give different dates: Wikipedia says it was first built in 1840 and wholly replaced in 1873; Connecticut History says the towns rebuilt it in 1873 for $4,000 after nearly a century of an earlier crossing; the East Hampton trail brochure says built 1791 and rebuilt 1860. A truck broke through the floor in the 1920s and the Civilian Conservation Corps restored it in the 1930s, adding the gates.',
  'The board-sided covered span with a shorter open approach span to the east bank, on rubblestone and cut-granite abutments. It is closed to cars and open to walkers.',
  '',
  'In Salmon River State Forest (CT DEEP), open daily 8 a.m. to sunset. Small lot beside the bridge on Comstock Bridge Road off Route 16.',
  [W('Comstock\'s Bridge'),SRSF,['Connecticut History: the Comstock Bridge','https://connecticuthistory.org/the-comstock-bridge-brings-east-hampton-residents-together/'],ALT],
  conf='mixed',th=[41.55317,-72.4494],thn='Lot on Comstock Bridge Road',brick='no'),
N('rapallo-viaduct-east-hampton','Rapallo Viaduct','East Hampton','ctsw','rail','traces',41.57194,-72.47194,'site','public','walk','','1873–',
  'A wrought-iron viaduct built in 1873 for the New Haven, Middletown and Willimantic Railroad (the Air Line) across the Flat Brook valley, 1,380 feet long and 60 feet high according to the town\'s trail brochure. Its chief engineer doubted the iron bridges on the line, but an inspector passed them. In 1912–13 the New Haven Railroad put the brook in a culvert and dumped sand from the tracks until the viaduct was buried. Trains stopped in 1965 and the line became the Air Line trail.',
  'The iron is still inside the embankment, intact when a sewer line was dug in 1979; what you see is the high fill carrying the trail across the marsh. With the Lyman Viaduct a mile and a half east in Colchester it is one of the two oldest-type wrought-iron bridges left in the state, saved because they were buried.',
  'A whole iron railroad bridge hidden inside the embankment you walk on.',
  'Air Line State Park Trail (CT DEEP; free, dogs on leash). East Hampton\'s brochure says the trail is open dawn to dusk to non-motorized users and names the Cranberry Meadow trailhead on Smith Street (DEEP: Smith Street near Flanders Road); walk east along the trail, roughly a kilometre. The trailhead lot was not pinned: the lot mapped on Smith Street is a school\'s.',
  [W('Rapallo Viaduct'),ALT,ALSPT],
  brick='no'),
N('portland-brownstone-quarries','Portland Brownstone Quarries','Portland','ctsw','industry','water',41.57556,-72.64333,'site','permit','walk','','1690–1938',
  'James Stanclift began quarrying here in 1690, and commercial quarrying began in 1783. At the height of the brownstone era over 1,500 men worked the quarries, which shipped stone on their own vessels to New York, Boston, Philadelphia, Chicago and other cities. A flood in 1936 and the 1938 hurricane flooded the pits, which could never be drained again. A National Historic Landmark since 2000; the town bought the quarries in 1999–2000.',
  'The flooded quarries with their sheer brownstone walls. The site is now leased to a commercial adventure park (Brownstone Exploration & Discovery Park) with swimming, cliff jumping, scuba diving and zip lines across the pits.',
  '',
  'Town-owned, run by the park operator: a paid day pass in the summer season; hours not checked. Outside the season and the park\'s hours the quarries are closed.',
  [W('Portland Brownstone Quarries'),['Brownstone Exploration & Discovery Park','https://www.brownstonepark.com/']],
  th=[41.57433,-72.64545],thn='Lot on Brownstone Avenue',brick='no'),
]

